<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Willibrord</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 658–739</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Saint Willibrord</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Willibrord was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary from Northumbria, known as the Apostle of the Frisians. Arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> in 690 with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a>, he was consecrated archbishop of the Frisians by Pope Sergius I in 695 and made Utrecht his see. He also founded the abbey of Echternach in present-day Luxembourg, where he died and is buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/willibrord.jpg" alt="Willibrord" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georg_Sturm,_Willibrordus_predikt_het_Christendom_aan_de_Friezen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
